    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec requires validating an E.164 telephone's **national-number length** against its country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting with 400 otherwise. The new test in `AcceptanceTest` posts `\"+61 123\"` and expects a `400`.\n\nI implemented this in the existing normalizer `OwnerTelephone.toE164` (`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephone.java`), which was the natural place \u2014 it already produces the E.164 `digits` string and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (handled as 400 by `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`).\n\nChanges:\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map (`\"61\"\u21929`, `\"1\"\u219210`), a `LinkedHashMap` ordered **longest code first** so the longest matching prefix wins.\n- After the existing generic 8\u201315 total-digit check, the code finds the matching country prefix and rejects when `digits.length() - code.length()` doesn't equal the required national length. Unrecognized country codes fall through to the generic rule unchanged.\n- Updated the Javadoc to document the per-country rule.\n\nThis is stricter than the old generic check in the way that matters: e.g. `+1` with 9 national digits (11 total) passed the old 8\u201315 rule but is now correctly rejected. The valid fixtures still pass \u2014 `ownerNode()`'s `04XXXXXXXX` normalizes to `+61` + 9 national digits.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` \u2014 passes.\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
